the body of the latest victim of alleged  police brutality in chile  lies in wake in a low-income  neighborhood of santiago  twenty-seven-year-old francisco martinez  romero was a juggler  a street artist who traveled around  south america but who met his end  in a small lakeside town in his home  country  since he was a child he loved using his  hands juggling  he was an artist who never hurt anyone  and who used harmless tin swords for his  act  martinez was shot dead after he charged  a policeman  who had first fired two warning shots at  him during an argument over a routine  identity check  it's unleashed a new wave of outrage and  vandalism  from southern chile to the capital  while his body lay in wake street  artists paid homage to martinez  the delegation of indigenous mapuches  received special  applause to kill francisco in broad  daylight in front of a crowd of people  is  not an isolated incident it's part of  the police's modus operandi  in what appears to be a coincidence  martinez's family says the victim was  the uncle of a 16 year old protester  who was nearly killed last october when  a policeman threw him over a bridge  into an almost dry river  as then the government has asked police  for an exhaustive  and quick investigation into the  incident  people have been arriving all day from  all over the capitol not just to express  their condolences to the family but also  their determination to continue  demonstrating  until there is justice but given that  the policeman who fired the five shots  is insisting it was  in self-defense there's no guarantee at  this stage  that there will be the verdict that all  the people here are demanding  human rights monitors say only a  fraction of the hundreds of officers  accused of abuses  have been tried and sentenced and guilty  or not  this killing has only added more fuel to  the ongoing social unrest  in chile often flamed by allegations of  police brutality  lucia newman al jazeera santiago
 

